-- Country — ISO 3166-1 country with dial code, currency, language, and
-- optional geographic centroid / geometry. Plus the auxiliary atoms the Country
-- record's coordinate fields lean on.
--
-- Reference rows live in country-data.fenel as per-row value-decls
-- (`country_US = Country {…}`), indexed under `Country` by the value-registry
-- loader and registered as global-scope by-type dispatch targets
-- (storage-as-effect Phase 3a — the retired setStaticData map + countries.ts
-- array). A country's flag emoji is the pure `std:countryFlag` projection over
-- its `code` (storage-as-effect Phase 3c — the baked `_flag` row field was
-- dropped; the reference-data view materializes it by calling the projection).
-- Geography is supplied separately by the geo-centroids / geo-boundaries
-- field providers; reference rows omit it until available. Maybe records this
-- absence, while its Undefinable codec preserves the existing omitted/raw
-- provider-field representation without invented coordinates or boundaries.
-- Identity (label / description / icon) is `.fenel`-authored via the
-- `@codeName`/`@label`/`@description`/`@icon`/`@fieldLabel` directives below.
--
-- Phase 3b of the system-entities sweep — see
-- workstreams/ts-elimination/briefs/phase-3b-system-entities-small-batch.md.
-- This is the first system-entity .fenel file with multiple
-- top-level type-decls — the pattern mirrors `paths.fenel` (Phase 2.9 /
-- 2.10) which carries 7 decls in a single file. The four decls below
-- live in dependency order for human readers (Latitude / Longitude
-- referenced by GeoPoint; GeoPoint referenced by Country.geometry);
-- the loader exposes all four by id, so reference order doesn't matter
-- to the parser.
--
-- Latitude / Longitude are refined-Number atoms with the coordinate-
-- range bounds (path (c) of the brief's Watch-out §4). The pre-3b TS
-- shape kept the bounds inline on `centroid.lat` / `centroid.lon` via a
-- refined-Number-with-$interval literal; extracting them as named atoms
-- makes the bounds reusable (geometry / future bbox / great-circle
-- helpers can reference Latitude / Longitude directly) and dodges the
-- "predicate-refinement at field-position" grammar question.
--
-- GeoPoint is the named record that Country.geometry uses for its
-- inner shape. Country.centroid keeps the inline `{ lat: Latitude,
-- lon: Longitude }` record inside Maybe so its Latitude / Longitude
-- facets can select the concrete `centroid.Just._0.lat` / `.lon` paths
-- and retain their original field identities. Country.geometry uses
-- `List (List GeoPoint)` instead of a
-- doubly-anonymous nested record because (a) anonymous records inside
-- `List (List …)` parse awkwardly, and (b) the geometry shape is
-- repeated enough across spatial code to deserve a name. The
-- inline-vs-named asymmetry is intentional: centroid has override
-- targets (the labels), geometry doesn't.

@entity
@label "Latitude"
@description "Refined Number in [-90, 90]"
Latitude = Number | value ∈ [-90..90]
@entity
@label "Longitude"
@description "Refined Number in [-180, 180]"
Longitude = Number | value ∈ [-180..180]

@entity
GeoPoint = GeoPoint lat: Latitude lon: Longitude

@entity
@codeName "country"
@label "Country"
@description "ISO 3166-1 country with dial code, currency, and language"
@icon "flag"
@fieldLabel "alpha3" "Alpha-3"
@fieldReusable "alpha3"
@fieldLabel "centroid.Just._0.lat" "Latitude"
@fieldLabel "centroid.Just._0.lon" "Longitude"
Country = Country code: String alpha3: String name: String dialCode: String currency: String language: String centroid: Maybe ({ lat: Latitude, lon: Longitude }) geometry: Maybe (List (List GeoPoint))
